Detailed Criteria for Revocation of Designation (Related to Article 8-4 (1))
ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE REGIONAL CULTURE PROMOTION ACT [ATTACHED TABLE 1] <Newly Inserted on Jun. 22, 2021>

Detailed Criteria for Revocation of Designation (Related to Article 8-4 (1))

General Criteria

(a) Where there are two or more violations and the respective applicable criteria for administrative dispositions differ, the more severe criteria shall apply.

(b) The levels of the individual criteria for administrative dispositions apply where an administrative disposition has been imposed for the same violation within the most recent one-year period. In such cases, the applicable level of the individual criteria shall be determined based on the date of the administrative disposition and the date on which the same violation committed after that disposition was detected.

(c) Where the disposition authority is required to revoke a designation on the grounds of a second or subsequent violation, and the violation is recognized as resulting from minor, unintentional carelessness or error, the authority may treat it as a first violation and apply the corresponding disposition criteria. This shall not apply, however, to cases falling under Article 13-4 (1) 1 of the Act.

2. Individual Criteria

| Violation | Statutory basis | Disposition for first violation | Disposition for second or subsequent violation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| (a) Obtaining designation by fraud or other improper means | Article 13-4 (1) 1 of the Act | Revocation of designation | Revocation of designation |
| (b) Failure to meet the requirements for designation under Article 10 (4), Article 13 (3), or Article 13-3 (4) of the Act | Article 13-4 (1) 2 of the Act | Warning; provided that designation shall be revoked if the violation is not rectified within the period specified by the disposition authority | Revocation of designation |
| (c) Other cases in which acts detrimental to the public interest make it difficult to continue performing the duties of the designated institution | Article 13-4 (1) 3 of the Act | Warning; provided that designation shall be revoked if the violation is not rectified within the period specified by the disposition authority | Revocation of designation |

Note: When issuing a warning, the disposition authority shall specify a period of at least one month and inform the person subject to the disposition that the violation must be rectified within that period.

Criteria for Imposing Administrative Fines (Related to Article 24)
ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE REGIONAL CULTURE PROMOTION ACT [ATTACHED TABLE 2] <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>

Criteria for Imposing Administrative Fines (Related to Article 24)

General Criteria

(a) The imposition criteria according to the number of violations shall apply where an administrative fine has been imposed for the same violation within the most recent one-year period. In such cases, the number of violations shall be calculated based on the date on which the administrative fine was imposed for the same violation and the date on which the same violation was detected again (limited to a violation committed after that disposition).

(b) A Mayor/Do Governor or the mayor of a large city may reduce the administrative fine prescribed in the individual criteria under subparagraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases. This shall not apply, however, to a violator who is in arrears in paying administrative fines.
1) Where the violation results from minor negligence or carelessness.
2) Where no administrative fine has been imposed under the Regional Culture Promotion Act within the most recent three years.
3) Other cases in which a reduction of the administrative fine is deemed necessary, taking into account the degree, motives and consequences of the violation and other relevant circumstances.

(c) A Mayor/Do Governor or the mayor of a large city may increase the administrative fine prescribed in the individual criteria under subparagraph 2 by up to one-half in any of the following cases. Even when increased, the administrative fine shall not exceed the upper limit prescribed in Article 24 (1) of the Act.
1) Where the violation is recognized as intentional or resulting from gross negligence.
2) Where the nature and degree of the violation are serious and are recognized as causing substantial harm to the zone of culture.
3) Other cases in which an increase of the administrative fine is deemed necessary, taking into account the degree, motives and consequences of the violation and other relevant circumstances.

